{"type":"message","id":"47827a31","parentId":"c34d0c56","timestamp":"2026-03-28T12:52:34.975Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so the structure is:```typescript\nconst SomeSchema = z.object({\n  suppressToolErrors: z.boolean().optional(),\n  tts: TtsConfigSchema,  // NOT optional!}).strict().optional();\n```\n\nAnd `TtsConfigSchema` includes `edge: TtsMicrosoftConfigSchema`. The `TtsMicrosoftConfigSchema` is defined as:\n```typescript\nconst TtsMicrosoftConfigSchema = z.object({  enabled: z.boolean().optional(),\n  voice: z.string().optional(),  lang: z.string().optional(),\n  outputFormat: z.string().optional(),\n  pitch: z.string().optional(),\n  rate: z.string().optional(),  volume: z.string().optional(),\n  saveSubtitles: z.boolean().optional(),\n  proxy: z.string().optional(),\n  timeoutMs: z.number().int().min(1000).max(120000).optional(),}).strict().optional();\n\n\n```\n\nSo `edge` should accept an object with those optional fields and reject unknown keys. The validation error about `messages.tts.edge` suggests either a stale build or the schema definition itself has an issue. I need to check if `messages` is wrapped with `.strict()` somewhere upstream, which would reject the entire `tts.edge` subtree regardless of its internal structure.\n\nLooking at the issue more carefully, the problem seems to be that when the user provides `messages: { tts: { edge: {...} } }`, the schema is rejecting it even though `edge` is defined as a valid field. Let me trace through how `messages` is actually defined in the schema to see if there's a mismatch between what the user is sending and what the schema expects.","thinkingSignature":"9c8eb5f0f36b140e76d1bf43ac95e2e1a68c49d56ef3dcd57bf86a0528f446e4"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_ox9t3hxc8gpl_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -rn \"messages\\b\" /root/openclaw-fork/src/config/zod-schema.core.ts | head -20"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","mod