{"type":"message","id":"344c4081","parentId":"25532e8c","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:10:52.593Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, there's a long list of issues. Let me look at some specific ones:\n\n1. #56369 - gateway:startup hook fires before channel adapters are ready - this is about timing/initialization order\n2. #56367 - Voice-call plugin ignores sandbox.mode: \"off\" - forces Docker sandbox - this could be a simple config check bug\n3. #56341 - message_sent hook not firing for Telegram - I looked at this earlier\n4. #56338 - already has PR #56384 (fixed)\n5. #56321 - @openclaw/whatsapp not available on NPM - not a code bug\n\n\n6. #56315 - Slash commands route to wrong topic - routing logic bug\n7. #56306 - Telegram read tool for images fails to deliver - image delivery issue\n\nLooking at what would be straightforward to fix: the Telegram image delivery issue (#56306) looks promising since the internal read succeeds but delivery fails - that's a clear error handling or transport problem. The slash commands routing (#56315) is also straightforward since it's just a topic selection issue. Let me start with #56306.","thinkingSignature":"ce55de6186fd9cf399ee3048a02f3c2c1d12f6f941f5e292448a16a43761afac"},{"type":"text","text":"#56306（Telegram read tool 图片成功读取但投递失败）描述清晰。\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_xa7pjouku0nw_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh issue view 56306 --repo openclaw/openclaw --json number,title,body,labels --jq '{number,title,labels:[.labels[].name],body}'"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":68710,"output":310,"cacheRead":22625,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":91645,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774703439343,"responseId":"0617064f7be1a47daeb988216f053b4c"}}
{"type":"message","id":"94fe0600","parentId":"344c4081","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:10:53.076Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_xa7pjouku0nw_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nRegression (worked before, now fails)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nWhen an agent is instructed to send an image file to a user on Telegram, the read(file=\\\"...\\\") tool is executed. The agent's internal logs show that the tool successfully reads the image file from its local storage. However, the image is never delivered to the end-user on Telegram. No error message is shown to the user; the message is simply dropped.\\n\\nThis issue has been reproduced with both pre-existing local files and with files downloaded from a public URL during the session. Text-based messages work correctly, but this core file-sending feature appears to be broken at the platform level.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Start a conversation with an OpenClaw agent via the Telegram channel.\\n2. Ensure an image file (e.g., image.jpg) is present in the agent's workspace.\\n3. Instruct the agent to send the image file (e.g., \\\"send me image.jpg\\\").\\n4. The agent executes the read(file=\\\"image.jpg\\\") tool.\\n5. Agent-side Observation: The agent receives a successful tool output, similar to {\\\"read_response\\\": {\\\"output\\\": \\\"Read image file [image/jpeg]\\\"}}. This indicates the file was read from disk correctly.\\n6. User-side Observation: The user in the Telegram client receives nothing. No image, no message, no error.\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\n1. Start a conversation with an OpenClaw agent via the Telegram channel.\\n2. Ensure an image file (e.g., image.jpg) is present in the agent's workspace.\\n3. Instruct the agent to send the image file (e.g., \\\"send me image.jpg\\\").\\n4. The agent executes the read(file=\\\"image.jpg\\\") tool.\\n5. Agent-side Observation: The agent receives a successful tool output, similar to {\\\"read_response\\\": {\\\"output\\\": \\\"Read image file [image/jpeg]\\\"}}. This indicates the file was read from disk correctly.\\n6. User-side Observation: The user in the Telegram client receives nothing. No image, no message, no error.\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\n• The read tool itself is not failing; it successfully processes the file from the agent's perspective.\\n• This is not a file permissions or access issue on the agent's side.\\n• This is not a general connectivity issue, as text messages are sent and received correctly.\\n• As a workaround, we successfully used the exec tool with curl to upload the image to a third-party file-sharing service (transfer.whalebone.io) and then shared the resulting URL. This confirms:\\n  • The image file itself is valid.\\n  • The agent's environment has network access for egress via curl.\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nubuntu\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm g \\n\\n### Model\\n\\ngemini 2.5 pro - vertex ai \\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nopenclaw -\\u003e google-vertex/gemini-2.5-pro\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\n_No response_\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\n\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\n_No response_\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\n_No response_\",\"labels\":[\"bug\",\"regression\"],\"number\":56306,\"title\":\"[Bug]:  Telegram: read tool for images succeeds internally but fails to deliver file to user.\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":437,"aggregated":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nRegression (worked before, now fails)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nWhen an agent is instructed to send an image file to a user on Telegram, the read(file=\\\"...\\\") tool is executed. The agent's internal logs show that the tool successfully reads the image file from its local storage. However, the image is never delivered to the end-user on Telegram. No error message is shown to the user; the message is simply dropped.\\n\\nThis issue has been reproduced with both pre-existing local files and with files downloaded from a public URL during the session. Text-based messages work correctly, but this core file-sending feature appears to be broken at the platform level.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Start a conversation with an OpenClaw agent via the Telegram channel.\\n2. Ensure an image file (e.g., image.jpg) is present in the agent's workspace.\\n3. Instruct the agent to send the image file (e.g., \\\"send me image.jpg\\\").\\n4. The agent executes the read(file=\\\"image.jpg\\\") tool.\\n5. Agent-side Observation: The agent receives a successful tool output, similar to {\\\"read_response\\\": {\\\"output\\\": \\\"Read image file [image/jpeg]\\\"}}. This indicates the file was read from disk correctly.\\n6. User-side Observation: The user in the Telegram client receives nothing. No image, no message, no error.\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\n1. Start a conversation with an OpenClaw agent via the Telegram channel.\\n2. Ensure an image file (e.g., image.jpg) is present in the agent's workspace.\\n3. Instruct the agent to send the image file (e.g., \\\"send me image.jpg\\\").\\n4. The agent executes the read(file=\\\"image.jpg\\\") tool.\\n5. Agent-side Observation: The agent receives a successful tool output, similar to {\\\"read_response\\\": {\\\"output\\\": \\\"Read image file [image/jpeg]\\\"}}. This indicates the file was read from disk correctly.\\n6. User-side Observation: The user in the Telegram client receives nothing. No image, no message, no error.\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\n• The read tool itself is not failing; it successfully processes the file from the agent's perspective.\\n• This is not a file permissions or access issue on the agent's side.\\n• This is not a general connectivity issue, as text messages are sent and received correctly.\\n• As a workaround, we successfully used the exec tool with curl to upload the image to a third-party file-sharing service (transfer.whalebone.io) and then shared the resulting URL. This confirms:\\n  • The image file itself is valid.\\n  • The agent's environment has network access for egress via curl.\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nubuntu\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm g \\n\\n### Model\\n\\ngemini 2.5 pro - vertex ai \\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nopenclaw -\\u003e google-vertex/gemini-2.5-pro\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\n_No response_\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\n\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\n_No response_\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\n_No response_\",\"labels\":[\"bug\",\"regression\"],\"number\":56306,\"title\":\"[Bug]:  Telegram: read tool for images succeeds internally but fails to deliver file to user.\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774703453044}}
{"type":"message","id":"d9b08dc9","parentId":"94fe0600","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:09.932Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a good bug to investigate. The issue is:- `read` tool successfully reads image from disk- But the image is never delivered to Telegram user- Text messages work fine- The image is just silently dropped\n\nThe problem is likely in how the `read` tool result is used to construct a Telegram message. If the `read` tool returns the content but the Telegram delivery code doesn't properly convert it to a Telegram media message, it would just be silently dropped.\n\nLet me look at how the Telegram channel handles file/image delivery. Specifically, how does it take the output of a `read` tool and send it as a Telegram message?\n\nActually, this involves a complex interaction between:\n1. The `read` tool (which returns file content as a tool result)\n\n\n2. The Telegram channel's reply construction logic (which needs to interpret file content as a media message)\n\nThis is quite complex to debug without the actual code. Let me look for a simpler bug.\n\nActually, I think the bug might be simpler. The `read` tool might return the file content but not in a format that the Telegram channel recognizes as something to send. The Telegram channel might be looking for a specific format (like a path to a file, or a specific tool result structure) rather than raw content.\n\nLet me look at #56367 - Voice-call plugin ignores agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \"off\" - this seems more isolated.\n\nLet me check the voice-call plugin's sandbox configuration handling to see where the issue lies.","thinkingSignature":"8bdf5de57bf0eb18e7974b3cf5a5899d92d5e0aac1959cbb5a3264c60ae9bc3f"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_gzp2r6cw9vz4_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh issue view 56367 --repo openclaw/openclaw --json number,title,body --jq '{number,title,body}'"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":67066,"output":358,"cacheRead":22625,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":90049,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774703453080,"responseId":"0617065d359af0402ccd02654edb4ea5"}}
{"type":"message","id":"d9bb2781","parentId":"d9b08dc9","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:10.821Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_gzp2r6cw9vz4_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nBehavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nThe voice-call plugin's `generateVoiceResponse()` creates a Docker sandbox container for voice lane responses even when `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode` is set to `\\\"off\\\"`. The sandbox then fails because bind mounts from other agents (whatsapp, workspace) point to `/workspace/` which is outside the voice lane's `workspace-main` root. Workaround: adding an explicit `\\\"main\\\"` agent to `agents.list` with `sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"` resolves the issue.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Configure `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"` (or `\\\"all\\\"`)\\n2. Add two agents in `agents.list` (e.g., whatsapp and workspace) with `sandbox.mode: \\\"all\\\"` and custom `docker.binds` pointing to `/workspace/` subdirectories\\n3. Do NOT add an explicit \\\"main\\\" agent to `agents.list`\\n4. Configure voice-call plugin with `inboundPolicy: \\\"allowlist\\\"` and streaming enabled\\n5. Initiate a conversation-mode voice call: `openclaw gateway call voicecall.initiate --params '{\\\"to\\\":\\\"+1234567890\\\",\\\"message\\\":\\\"Hello\\\",\\\"mode\\\":\\\"conversation\\\"}'`\\n6. Speak to the caller — STT transcribes correctly but response generation fails with: `Error: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"...workspace/homebrew...\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)`\\n7. Adding `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"sandbox\\\":{\\\"mode\\\":\\\"off\\\"}}` to `agents.list` fixes the issue immediately\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\n`agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"` should disable Docker sandboxing for all sessions that don't have an explicit per-agent override, including voice lane sessions created by the voice-call plugin. Bind mounts from per-agent sandbox configs (whatsapp, workspace) should not be merged into sessions belonging to a different agent (main/voice).\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\n`resolveSandboxContext()` creates a Docker container for the voice lane despite `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"`. It also merges bind mounts from other agents in `agents.list` (whatsapp, workspace) into the voice session. The sandbox validation then fails with:\\nError: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:ro\\\" source \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)\\nStack trace points to `formatBindBlockedError` → `validateSandboxSecurity` → `buildSandboxCreateArgs` → `createSandboxContainer` → `ensureSandboxContainer` → `createDockerSandboxBackend` → `resolveSandboxContext`.\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nUbuntu 24.04 (DigitalOcean VPS)\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Model\\n\\nopenai/gpt-4o-mini (voice-call default responseModel)\\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nopenclaw -\\u003e openai (response generation), twilio (telephony), elevenlabs (TTS), openai realtime (STT)\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\nvoice-call plugin config: provider twilio, TTS via elevenlabs (eleven_multilingual_v2), STT via OpenAI Realtime API. responseModel defaults to openai/gpt-4o-mini. The plugin's generateVoiceResponse() uses agentId=\\\"main\\\" and sessionKey=\\\"voice:\\u003cphone_digits\\u003e\\\", then calls agentRuntime.runEmbeddedPiAgent() with lane=\\\"voice\\\". The voice lane creates workspace-main instead of using the configured workspace directory.\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\nGateway log showing sandbox error on voice response:\\n\\n[voice-call] Auto-responding to inbound call ec0453c8: \\\"Hola Nova, ¿cuál es la temperatura de Gibraltar hoy?\\\"\\n[diagnostic] lane task error: lane=voice durationMs=134 error=\\\"Error: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:ro\\\" source \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)\\\"\\n[voice-call] Response generation failed: Error: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"...\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)\\n    at formatBindBlockedError (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:994:77)\\n    at validateSandboxSecurity (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1052:2)\\n    at buildSandboxCreateArgs (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1272:2)\\n    at createSandboxContainer (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1324:15)\\n    at async ensureSandboxContainer (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1402:21)\\n    at async createDockerSandboxBackend (sandbox-8wi_NBXO.js:226:18)\\n    at async resolveSandboxContext (sandbo-8wi_NBXO.js:2615:18)\\n\\nConfig state when bug occurs:\\n- agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"\\n- agents.defaults.sandbox.docker.binds: removed (only network: \\\"bridge\\\")\\n- agents.list[whatsapp].sandbox.mode: \\\"all\\\" with binds\\n- agents.list[workspace].sandbox.mode: \\\"all\\\" with binds\\n- No explicit \\\"main\\\" agent in agents.list\\n\\nAfter adding {\\\"id\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"sandbox\\\":{\\\"mode\\\":\\\"off\\\"}} to agents.list, voice calls work immediately.\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\nAffected: Any user running voice-call plugin with multi-agent sandbox configs\\nSeverity: High (completely blocks voice-call conversation mode responses)\\nFrequency: 100% — every conversation-mode voice call fails\\nConsequence: Voice calls connect and STT works, but the agent never responds. Caller hears silence after speaking. Workaround exists (add explicit main agent to agents.list).\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\nThe root cause appears to be in resolveSandboxContext() — it does not respect agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\" when the \\\"main\\\" agent is implicit (not in agents.list). The voice-call plugin's generateVoiceResponse() in response-generator-30TP-Ojl.js hardcodes agentId=\\\"main\\\" and creates sessionKey=\\\"voice:\\u003cdigits\\u003e\\\". When \\\"main\\\" is not explicitly listed, resolveSandboxContext() appears to merge sandbox config (including docker.binds) from other agents in the list. Additionally, the voice lane creates its own workspace directory (workspace-main) instead of using the configured workspace, causing bind mount validation to fail even if sandbox mode were correctly resolved. Workaround confirmed on v2026.3.24: adding {\\\"id\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"sandbox\\\":{\\\"mode\\\":\\\"off\\\"}} to agents.list immediately fixes voice-call conversation mode.\",\"number\":56367,\"title\":\"[Bug]: Voice-call plugin ignores agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\" - forces Docker sandbox for voice lane responses\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":844,"aggregated":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nBehavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nThe voice-call plugin's `generateVoiceResponse()` creates a Docker sandbox container for voice lane responses even when `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode` is set to `\\\"off\\\"`. The sandbox then fails because bind mounts from other agents (whatsapp, workspace) point to `/workspace/` which is outside the voice lane's `workspace-main` root. Workaround: adding an explicit `\\\"main\\\"` agent to `agents.list` with `sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"` resolves the issue.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Configure `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"` (or `\\\"all\\\"`)\\n2. Add two agents in `agents.list` (e.g., whatsapp and workspace) with `sandbox.mode: \\\"all\\\"` and custom `docker.binds` pointing to `/workspace/` subdirectories\\n3. Do NOT add an explicit \\\"main\\\" agent to `agents.list`\\n4. Configure voice-call plugin with `inboundPolicy: \\\"allowlist\\\"` and streaming enabled\\n5. Initiate a conversation-mode voice call: `openclaw gateway call voicecall.initiate --params '{\\\"to\\\":\\\"+1234567890\\\",\\\"message\\\":\\\"Hello\\\",\\\"mode\\\":\\\"conversation\\\"}'`\\n6. Speak to the caller — STT transcribes correctly but response generation fails with: `Error: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"...workspace/homebrew...\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)`\\n7. Adding `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"sandbox\\\":{\\\"mode\\\":\\\"off\\\"}}` to `agents.list` fixes the issue immediately\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\n`agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"` should disable Docker sandboxing for all sessions that don't have an explicit per-agent override, including voice lane sessions created by the voice-call plugin. Bind mounts from per-agent sandbox configs (whatsapp, workspace) should not be merged into sessions belonging to a different agent (main/voice).\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\n`resolveSandboxContext()` creates a Docker container for the voice lane despite `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"`. It also merges bind mounts from other agents in `agents.list` (whatsapp, workspace) into the voice session. The sandbox validation then fails with:\\nError: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:ro\\\" source \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)\\nStack trace points to `formatBindBlockedError` → `validateSandboxSecurity` → `buildSandboxCreateArgs` → `createSandboxContainer` → `ensureSandboxContainer` → `createDockerSandboxBackend` → `resolveSandboxContext`.\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nUbuntu 24.04 (DigitalOcean VPS)\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Model\\n\\nopenai/gpt-4o-mini (voice-call default responseModel)\\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nopenclaw -\\u003e openai (response generation), twilio (telephony), elevenlabs (TTS), openai realtime (STT)\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\nvoice-call plugin config: provider twilio, TTS via elevenlabs (eleven_multilingual_v2), STT via OpenAI Realtime API. responseModel defaults to openai/gpt-4o-mini. The plugin's generateVoiceResponse() uses agentId=\\\"main\\\" and sessionKey=\\\"voice:\\u003cphone_digits\\u003e\\\", then calls agentRuntime.runEmbeddedPiAgent() with lane=\\\"voice\\\". The voice lane creates workspace-main instead of using the configured workspace directory.\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\nGateway log showing sandbox error on voice response:\\n\\n[voice-call] Auto-responding to inbound call ec0453c8: \\\"Hola Nova, ¿cuál es la temperatura de Gibraltar hoy?\\\"\\n[diagnostic] lane task error: lane=voice durationMs=134 error=\\\"Error: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew:ro\\\" source \\\"/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace/homebrew\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)\\\"\\n[voice-call] Response generation failed: Error: Sandbox security: bind mount \\\"...\\\" is outside allowed roots (/home/openclaw/.openclaw/workspace-main)\\n    at formatBindBlockedError (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:994:77)\\n    at validateSandboxSecurity (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1052:2)\\n    at buildSandboxCreateArgs (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1272:2)\\n    at createSandboxContainer (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1324:15)\\n    at async ensureSandboxContainer (docker-BrzH-NvE.js:1402:21)\\n    at async createDockerSandboxBackend (sandbox-8wi_NBXO.js:226:18)\\n    at async resolveSandboxContext (sandbo-8wi_NBXO.js:2615:18)\\n\\nConfig state when bug occurs:\\n- agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\"\\n- agents.defaults.sandbox.docker.binds: removed (only network: \\\"bridge\\\")\\n- agents.list[whatsapp].sandbox.mode: \\\"all\\\" with binds\\n- agents.list[workspace].sandbox.mode: \\\"all\\\" with binds\\n- No explicit \\\"main\\\" agent in agents.list\\n\\nAfter adding {\\\"id\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"sandbox\\\":{\\\"mode\\\":\\\"off\\\"}} to agents.list, voice calls work immediately.\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\nAffected: Any user running voice-call plugin with multi-agent sandbox configs\\nSeverity: High (completely blocks voice-call conversation mode responses)\\nFrequency: 100% — every conversation-mode voice call fails\\nConsequence: Voice calls connect and STT works, but the agent never responds. Caller hears silence after speaking. Workaround exists (add explicit main agent to agents.list).\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\nThe root cause appears to be in resolveSandboxContext() — it does not respect agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\" when the \\\"main\\\" agent is implicit (not in agents.list). The voice-call plugin's generateVoiceResponse() in response-generator-30TP-Ojl.js hardcodes agentId=\\\"main\\\" and creates sessionKey=\\\"voice:\\u003cdigits\\u003e\\\". When \\\"main\\\" is not explicitly listed, resolveSandboxContext() appears to merge sandbox config (including docker.binds) from other agents in the list. Additionally, the voice lane creates its own workspace directory (workspace-main) instead of using the configured workspace, causing bind mount validation to fail even if sandbox mode were correctly resolved. Workaround confirmed on v2026.3.24: adding {\\\"id\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"sandbox\\\":{\\\"mode\\\":\\\"off\\\"}} to agents.list immediately fixes voice-call conversation mode.\",\"number\":56367,\"title\":\"[Bug]: Voice-call plugin ignores agents.defaults.sandbox.mode: \\\"off\\\" - forces Docker sandbox for voice lane responses\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774703470790}}
{"type":"message","id":"d942ca45","parentId":"d9bb2781","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:18.472Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a complex bug involving:\n1. `resolveSandboxContext()` not respecting `agents.defaults.sandbox.mode`\n2. Voice-call plugin hardcoding `agentId=\"main\"`\n3. Bind mount validation failing\n\nThis is deep inside the sandbox system. I should find something simpler.\n\nLet me look at #56315 (Slash commands route to wrong topic) - this is about routing logic.","thinkingSignature":"10715517fc8078029e441d196d6d5b62a7ad3f2359a0eb7d39a5e07893f488db"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_m20xthj54ygr_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh issue view 56315 --repo openclaw/openclaw --json number,title,body --jq '{number,title,body}'"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":66355,"output":132,"cacheRead":22625,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":89112,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774703470825,"responseId":"0617066eedfccf5d875516754dc1c99d"}}
{"type":"message","id":"1756c067","parentId":"d942ca45","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:18.939Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_m20xthj54ygr_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nRegression (worked before, now fails)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n### Bug type\\nBehavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)\\n\\n### Summary\\nIn a Telegram forum supergroup with a single bot, slash commands like /reset, /new, /status, and /reasoning sent in a specific topic are routed to the General topic instead of being executed in the current topic's session context.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n1. Telegram supergroup with forum topics enabled, single bot\\n2. Open any non-General topic\\n3. Send /reset (or /status, /reasoning, /new)\\n4. Command is processed in General, not in the current topic\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\nSlash commands should execute in the context of the topic where they are sent.\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\nCommands route to General. The only workaround is using the OpenClaw dashboard to manage topic-specific sessions.\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\nmacOS (Darwin arm64)\\n\\n### Install method\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Additional information\\nRelated to #35963 but that issue involves a 2-bot setup. This reproduces with a single bot — the threadId is not applied to slash command routing regardless of bot count.\\n\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Open a Telegram forum supergroup with a single bot\\n2. Navigate to any non-General topic\\n3. Send /reset, /new, /status, or /reasoning\\n4. Observe the command is executed in General topic, not the current topic\\n\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\nSlash commands should execute in the session context of the topic where they are sent. A /reset in topic X should reset topic X's session.\\n\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\nAll slash commands are routed to the General topic regardless of which topic they are sent in. To manage a specific topic's session, the user must use the OpenClaw dashboard.\\n\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nmacOS 15 (Darwin 24.6.0 arm64)\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Model\\n\\nanthropic/claude-opus-4-6\\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nopenclaw -\\u003e anthropic (direct, API key)\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\nSingle bot setup. Direct Anthropic API, no proxy or router. Config in ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json.\\n\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\n\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\nAffects all Telegram forum supergroup users. Severity: blocks workflow — unable to manage sessions per topic from Telegram, forced to use dashboard as workaround. Happens 100% of the time with any slash command in any non-General topic.\\n\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\n_No response_\",\"number\":56315,\"title\":\"[Bug]: Slash commands (/reset, /status, /reasoning) route to General topic instead of current forum topic (single bot)\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":388,"aggregated":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nRegression (worked before, now fails)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n### Bug type\\nBehavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)\\n\\n### Summary\\nIn a Telegram forum supergroup with a single bot, slash commands like /reset, /new, /status, and /reasoning sent in a specific topic are routed to the General topic instead of being executed in the current topic's session context.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n1. Telegram supergroup with forum topics enabled, single bot\\n2. Open any non-General topic\\n3. Send /reset (or /status, /reasoning, /new)\\n4. Command is processed in General, not in the current topic\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\nSlash commands should execute in the context of the topic where they are sent.\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\nCommands route to General. The only workaround is using the OpenClaw dashboard to manage topic-specific sessions.\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\nmacOS (Darwin arm64)\\n\\n### Install method\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Additional information\\nRelated to #35963 but that issue involves a 2-bot setup. This reproduces with a single bot — the threadId is not applied to slash command routing regardless of bot count.\\n\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Open a Telegram forum supergroup with a single bot\\n2. Navigate to any non-General topic\\n3. Send /reset, /new, /status, or /reasoning\\n4. Observe the command is executed in General topic, not the current topic\\n\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\nSlash commands should execute in the session context of the topic where they are sent. A /reset in topic X should reset topic X's session.\\n\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\nAll slash commands are routed to the General topic regardless of which topic they are sent in. To manage a specific topic's session, the user must use the OpenClaw dashboard.\\n\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nmacOS 15 (Darwin 24.6.0 arm64)\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Model\\n\\nanthropic/claude-opus-4-6\\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nopenclaw -\\u003e anthropic (direct, API key)\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\nSingle bot setup. Direct Anthropic API, no proxy or router. Config in ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json.\\n\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\n\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\nAffects all Telegram forum supergroup users. Severity: blocks workflow — unable to manage sessions per topic from Telegram, forced to use dashboard as workaround. Happens 100% of the time with any slash command in any non-General topic.\\n\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\n_No response_\",\"number\":56315,\"title\":\"[Bug]: Slash commands (/reset, /status, /reasoning) route to General topic instead of current forum topic (single bot)\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774703478916}}
{"type":"message","id":"bfc8bc1b","parentId":"1756c067","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:29.475Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"#56315 is a clear bug - slash commands in Telegram forum topics route to General topic instead of the current topic. This is related to how `threadId` is handled for slash command routing.\n\nThe bug is: when a slash command is sent in a non-General topic, the `threadId` is not used for routing, so it falls back to General. The fix would be to include the `threadId` in the routing decision for slash commands.\n\nLet me look at how Telegram slash commands are handled.\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. Instead of deeply analyzing every bug, let me just look at a very simple bug and write a fix. The issue should be:\n\n\nXS/S in size with a straightforward code fix. Let me check if there's already a PR for #56315 that I can reference or build upon.","thinkingSignature":"0a66060a5f8ead9b1e925098603deb9930fc6944c507e4967355d078ad3571da"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_sh7xoij474yk_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh pr list --repo openclaw/openclaw --search \"56315\" --state all --json number,title --jq '.[]'"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":65075,"output":220,"cacheRead":22625,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":87920,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774703478943,"responseId":"061706770e556e3a76bcd984ac0be2b5"}}
{"type":"message","id":"cdd56822","parentId":"bfc8bc1b","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:29.993Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_sh7xoij474yk_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"number\":56337,\"title\":\"test(telegram): add regression test for slash command routing in forum topics\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":481,"aggregated":"{\"number\":56337,\"title\":\"test(telegram): add regression test for slash command routing in forum topics\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774703489969}}
{"type":"message","id":"f04d072f","parentId":"cdd56822","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:33.839Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"PR #56337 is a test-only PR for the slash command routing issue. This means the actual fix hasn't been written yet. Let me look at this PR.","thinkingSignature":"f14132db0afff0fffa3dcb3d227e2187f1027d20fcfa84f56ae1810b91370ac0"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_wuzgdg6uzoel_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/56337 --jq '{number,title,state,author:.author.login,body}'"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":65334,"output":85,"cacheRead":22625,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":88044,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774703490000,"responseId":"06170682d32cb4eef66104789e84e1f9"}}
{"type":"message","id":"9d8b60e9","parentId":"f04d072f","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:34.545Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_wuzgdg6uzoel_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"author\":null,\"body\":\"## What\\n\\nAdd a regression test locking in correct slash command routing in Telegram forum topics, preventing future regressions where `resolvedThreadId` could accidentally be dropped.\\n\\nFixes #56315\\n\\n## Finding\\n\\nAfter tracing the full slash command pipeline in `bot-native-commands.ts`, the routing logic was **already correct**: `resolvedThreadId` from the incoming forum message is properly propagated through `resolveCommandRuntimeContext` into `CommandTargetSessionKey`, `From`, and `MessageThreadId`. A `/reset` in topic 123 correctly produces session key `agent:main:telegram:group:-1001234567890:topic:123`, not the General topic session.\\n\\nThe bug reported is likely caused by a misconfiguration (e.g. `message_thread_id` absent from bot webhook setup, or `isForum` flag not set in config) rather than a code path error.\\n\\n## Test Added\\n\\n`extensions/telegram/src/bot-native-commands.session-meta.test.ts` — new test:\\n\\n\\u003e *\\\"routes /reset in forum topic to topic session, not General session (issue #56315)\\\"*\\n\\nAsserts:\\n- `CommandTargetSessionKey` = `agent:main:telegram:group:-1001234567890:topic:123` (not General)\\n- `From` carries the correct forum thread ID\\n- `IsForum = true`\\n\\nAll 1101 tests pass.\",\"number\":56337,\"state\":\"closed\",\"title\":\"test(telegram): add regression test for slash command routing in forum topics\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":674,"aggregated":"{\"author\":null,\"body\":\"## What\\n\\nAdd a regression test locking in correct slash command routing in Telegram forum topics, preventing future regressions where `resolvedThreadId` could accidentally be dropped.\\n\\nFixes #56315\\n\\n## Finding\\n\\nAfter tracing the full slash command pipeline in `bot-native-commands.ts`, the routing logic was **already correct**: `resolvedThreadId` from the incoming forum message is properly propagated through `resolveCommandRuntimeContext` into `CommandTargetSessionKey`, `From`, and `MessageThreadId`. A `/reset` in topic 123 correctly produces session key `agent:main:telegram:group:-1001234567890:topic:123`, not the General topic session.\\n\\nThe bug reported is likely caused by a misconfiguration (e.g. `message_thread_id` absent from bot webhook setup, or `isForum` flag not set in config) rather than a code path error.\\n\\n## Test Added\\n\\n`extensions/telegram/src/bot-native-commands.session-meta.test.ts` — new test:\\n\\n\\u003e *\\\"routes /reset in forum topic to topic session, not General session (issue #56315)\\\"*\\n\\nAsserts:\\n- `CommandTargetSessionKey` = `agent:main:telegram:group:-1001234567890:topic:123` (not General)\\n- `From` carries the correct forum thread ID\\n- `IsForum = true`\\n\\nAll 1101 tests pass.\",\"number\":56337,\"state\":\"closed\",\"title\":\"test(telegram): add regression test for slash command routing in forum topics\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774703494526}}
{"type":"message","id":"d3aac3d3","parentId":"9d8b60e9","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:11:53.610Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! PR #56337 was closed with the conclusion that the bug is NOT a code path error - the routing logic was already correct. The bug is likely caused by a misconfiguration (missing `message_thread_id` from webhook setup, or `isForum` flag not set in config).\n\nSo the actual bug is not in the code but in the configuration/webhook setup. This means there's no simple code fix to write.\n\nOK let me step back. I've been spending a lot of time trying to find a bug to write a PR for, and I'm not finding one that's both:1. In an area I understand\n2. Hasn't been fixed by someone else\n\n\n3. Is XS/S size\n\nInstead of continuing to hunt for the perfect bug, I should focus on what I actually know how to do well - supporting existing PRs, reviewing code, and analyzing issues. The PR #56337 was closed because the team concluded the routing logic is correct, so I should move past that approach. Let me reconsider #56369 about the gateway startup hook firing before channel adapters are ready, and whether the issue is actually about `gateway:start` versus `gateway:ready` timing.\n\nLooking at the two potential fixes: making the startup hook fire after adapters initialize would be a larger architectural change, while adding retry logic to the message tool is more isolated but requires understanding how errors are currently handled. Neither is straightforward. Maybe I should explore an issue in the docs instead - something like PR #56203 where I supported the sessions_send sessionKey/label handling, or look at something in the Feishu wiki domain entirely.\n\nActually, PR #56241 about edge TTS config validation might be worth investigating more carefully. The PR claims to add tests, but if the schema still doesn't accept `edge`, those tests would fail - so there might be a real underlying issue there.","thinkingSignature":"4f5eca50f408a52dc7032f180a34f358bc8a845077d9407d2f2ed1d856011edc"},{"type":"text","text":"好，#56315 已被调查后关闭（代码逻辑正确，问题出在 webhook 配置）。让我找另一个合适的 bug 来写 PR。\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_o0l0s39o2oef_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"edge\\|microsoft\" /root/openclaw-fork/src/config/zod-schema.core.ts | grep -v \"//\\|edge\\b\\|Microsoft\" | head -20"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":65119,"output":470,"cacheRead":22625,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":88214,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774703494550,"responseId":"06170686b58c9cb1b4c2e7b5832fc447"}}
