{"type":"message","id":"f9aaf19a","parentId":"a5f1400e","timestamp":"2026-03-28T13:18:13.134Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So #56241 only adds tests, it doesn't actually change the schema itself. That means the schema already accepts `messages.tts.edge` and the tests just verify that. But wait - if the tests were added, they should fail on the current main (before merge) and pass after. Unless the schema was already correct and the bug was just that there were no tests.\n\nLet me check the current main's schema for the edge field. We already know from our earlier exploration that `TtsConfigSchema` has `edge: TtsMicrosoftConfigSchema` in it. So the schema already accepts `edge`. The PR just adds tests.\n\nThis means the bug might be different from what I thought. Let me look at the actual issue #56220 to understand better.\n\n\nThe PR description clarifies the real issue: the config validation was rejecting `messages.tts.edge` as an unrecognized key, even though the documentation and TypeScript types both indicate it's a supported configuration option. The PR adds explicit tests to verify that `TtsConfigSchema` properly accepts this legacy configuration key.\n\nBut I'm noticing something odd—the schema already has `edge` defined in `TtsConfigSchema`, so the tests shouldn't be necessary unless the schema itself was recently corrected or there's a different path being validated. Let me check the file change to understand what's actually being added.\n\nWait, the issue mentions `messages.tts.edge` but in the schema `tts` is nested under `messages`, so the full path would be `messages.tts.edge`. If the user is configuring `channels.feishu.messages.tts.edge`, that might be a completely different location in the config structure. That's likely where the disconnect is.","thinkingSignature":"995c1d508a6cdda0d03867158f784ef75cc84257fac4adec3528470ef4ee0d34"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_4hpndtairdnx_1","name":"exec","argum